8f 00 00 00 00 00`. If we overwrite only first 2 bytes with gadget address low 16 bits, the remaining bytes from original remain: `?? ?? 8f 00 00 00 00 00`, which may not be what we want unless target address shares same high bits. We can overwrite low 3 bytes to set address to 0x0000000000xxxxxx. Return address original is 0x8ff555, so high byte 0x00 already. So overwrite first 3 bytes with e.g. system PLT 0x407450 bytes `50 74 40`, leaving 0x00... giving exactly 0x407450. Great! So only 3 non-NUL bytes needed to write `system@plt`, if original high bytes are zero. Then need set RDI to command pointer. Can't write ROP chain with addresses without NUL unless we use partial overwrites or use return address to a gadget that sets RDI from existing register/stack. Maybe `system@plt` with RDI already pointing to something attacker-controlled? At time of function return, RDI may still hold a pointer we can influence? At end of `pdfi_open_CIDFont_substitute_file`, before return, registers might contain useful values. We can inspect at line just before return. But RDI likely points to `fontfname` buffer or something. If RDI points to the buffer, `system()` could execute the command string stored in the buffer. Excellent! At line335, `pdfi_open_resource_file(ctx, fontfname, strlen(fontfname), &s)` likely sets RDI to fontfname. But after that call returns, RDI may be preserved? Not guaranteed. At function return, the disassembly epilogue may load/pop, but RDI could remain from prior call. We can inspect in GDB just before `ret` in function. The crash is before return. For original poc, if we stop at return instruction, inspect RDI. Let's find epilogue around end of function. `objdump` at end."}],"encrypted_content":null}}
